
My idea for MyGame came from the IoT project we did in Lab 7, where I had two ESP boards talking to each
other (one acting as a sensor node and the other acting as the receiver/server). I liked the hardware communication
I got to work with while building the IoT system, the delay in readings, and the random issues that
happened when the sensors didn’t respond properly. That inspired me to turn it into a console based game.

Below are the actual types of prompts and questions I asked the AI while building this project,
plus the good and bad parts of the experience and what I learned while trying to work with it.

The prompts I used are

- “How can I turn an IoT sensor network into a text/console game?”
- “What kind of options should I add so the user can manage the system”
- “I'm getting "x" as my error in this "sensor.java" file help me find the cause”
- “Show me how to structure my project”
- “Give me ideas that would trigger sensors and causes malfunctions on Mars”
- “Give me a rough example of a hierarchy if i want to have more than one sensor in the game”
- “How should i include numbers in javadocs and it wont be considered as magic numbers”

The best parts of using the AI

The AI helped me think of ideas faster. When I got stuck or blanked out, especially on how to design the hierarchy
for the different sensors, it gave me a push in the right direction. It didn’t give me perfect code, but the
suggestions made me think of what I wanted to write. I stilll rewrote everything to match Jason’s style because
the automatically generated stuff doesnt follow his rules.

Another good part was using it to simplify explanations. Sometimes I would overthink something like “how should
I validate the mode of the sensor?”, but the AI helped me realize I just needed an enum and one validator method.

The random event system also came from a few back and forth chats where I asked it to generate ideas, becuase I didnt
know what kind of "things" that can trigger sensors. That helped me shape what eventually became the dust storms,
radiation spikes, and solar boosts.

The worst parts and limitations
Sometimes the AI wanted to give code that used things we never learned and I didnt quite understand the full version.
I had to tell it multiple times that I can only use material we covered in class like streams, basic OOP, etc..
but even then, I still had to rewrite or throw away large portions of the suggestions because they didn’t match our
coding style and conventions.

Another issue was overcomplication, the AI would sometimes suggest features that seemed cool but didn’t belong in
my game scope, making a GUI, using input validation libraries, or adding extra classes that
made no sense. I had to keep reminding it that this was a simple, console game and not a large professional project.

Sometimes the explanations were very repetitive, or it tried to give me entire “pre-written” files that I didn’t
want because everything must be my own writing. I eventually just took small ideas from the prompts and implemented
the code manually in a way that fit my design.

What I learned from using the AI
I realized that AI is good at giving inspiration but not good at giving final code that fits strict course standards.
I had to constantly filter the output.